PREFIX pair:  <http://w3id.org/airiskkg/pair-ai#>
PREFIX facet: <http://w3id.org/airiskkg/facets#>
PREFIX dataf: <http://w3id.org/airiskkg/facets/data#>

# Bridge: OECD data-rights status -> PAIR-AI content category.
#
# Data carrying the OECD "Personal" rights status is, by pair:SensitiveInformation's
# own definition, protection-worthy content ("personal, medical, financial, or
# otherwise protection-worthy"). Both concepts also point at DPV independently -
# dataf:Personal skos:relatedMatch dpv:PersonalData, pair:SensitiveInformation
# skos:relatedMatch dpv:SensitivePersonalData - so the bridge is corroborated by a
# shared external vocabulary rather than asserted on our own authority.
#
# Why a bridge rather than a second alternative inside the risk queries:
#
#   1. It changes no existing query's semantics. Conditions keep reading one
#      vocabulary (pair:DataCategory) and gain the facet route for free.
#   2. It runs in the same fixed-point loop as the other propagation rules, so a
#      derived category then flows downstream through
#      propagation/content_categories.rq and stops at a pair:RedactionStep,
#      exactly like a hand-annotated one. Tagging the source is enough.
#   3. Adding the next facet -> category bridge costs one query, not an edit to
#      every condition that should honour it.
#
# Direction matters and is deliberate: an OECD rights status implies the content
# category, NOT the reverse. Sensitive information is a broader notion than
# personal data (trade secrets are sensitive and not personal), so deriving
# dataf:Personal from pair:SensitiveInformation would over-claim - it would assert
# data-protection status the modeler never stated.
#
# Rule R8: facet:hasDataRights is an annotated base fact supplied by the modeler;
# the category asserted here is derived.

CONSTRUCT {
    ?element pair:containsDataCategory pair:SensitiveInformation .
}
WHERE {
    ?element facet:hasDataRights dataf:Personal .
}
